## Selective Aerobic Oxidation of Metal-Organic Framework Boosts Thermodynamic and Kinetic Propylene/Propane Selectivity
As one of the most important chemicals in chemical industry, propylene \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\) is produced more than 100 million tons per year.[1] Most \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) is recovered from the C3 fraction of the cracked gas, in which the main byproduct is propane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\).[2] The separation of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) mixtures is performed by distillation at high pressure (20 bar). Due to the close boiling point of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) (226 K) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) (231 K),[3] their relative volatility is very small (1.09-1.15)[4] and the splitter should have a very large number of trays \((180+)\)[5] and be operated at very high reflux ratios (12-20)[6], resulting in huge energy consumptions.[1]
Without phase change, the energy consumption of adsorption is much lower than distillation.[7] In principle, adsorbents can distinguish guest molecules through size, shape, polarity, coordination ability, and other characteristics.[8] Adsorptive separation relies on thermodynamic and kinetic mechanisms, based on different guest adsorption affinity and diffusion rate, respectively. Porous coordination polymers (PCPs) or metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), with diversified, designable, and ordered structures, are promising adsorbents
for adsorptive separation.[1] The best \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) separation performances were achieved by using MOFs, such as MOF-74 with open metal site for selective coordination with the alkene,[10] and KAUST-7 with the molecular sieving effect.[11] However, desorption from the strong binding site is difficult. As an ideal and simple separation mechanism, molecular sieving is challenging for precise design/control of the pore size (even for MOFs), especially considering that porous frameworks (especially for MOFs) always possess more or less flexibility which alters the pore size.
Compared with direct synthesis, post-synthetic modification is more reliable for attaining the desired structure/property, but few MOFs are stable and reactive enough for such reactions.[12] Recently, we reported several ultramicroporous MOFs based on a flexible ligand bis(5-methyl-1H1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane \(\left(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t m}\right)\).[12b, 13] The flexibility of \(\left[\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{b t m})_{2}\right]\) (MAF-23) has been demonstrated to be essential for its exceptional C4-hydrocarbon separation because its aperture size \((3.6\mathrm{~\AA~})\) is smaller than the guests.[13b] MAF-23 showed poor selectivity for the smaller C2/C3 hydrocarbons,[13b] indicating the lack of suitable guest recognition site and excess of framework flexibility. Because the hydrophobic and soft methylene group in \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t m}\) is connected and activated by two aromatic rings, it may be oxidized to form the hydrophilic and rigid carbonyl group for enhancing guest recognition and framework rigidity, but oxidation catalysts such as Fe(III) or \(\mathsf{C u}(1)\) ions are necessary.[12b] Here, we show that without adding any external catalyst, half of the \(\mathsf{b t m}^{2-}\) ligands in MAF-23 can be selectively oxidized by oxygen to form \(\mathsf{b t k}^{2-}\) \(\left(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t k}=\mathsf{b i s}(5\mathrm{-methyl}\mathrm{-}1,2,4\mathrm{-triazol}\mathrm{-}3\mathrm{-yl})\right.\) methanone), resulting in drastic improvement of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) adsorption selectivity.
Although MAF-23 exhibits very high thermal stability under inert atmosphere,[13a] the colorless crystals turn yellow at high temperature in air. Heating MAF-23 at \(140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1500\ \mathrm{min}\) in oxygen flow yielded yellow crystals of \(\left[\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{b t m})(\mathsf{b t k})\right]\) hereafter denoted as MAF-23-O (Figures S1-S2), in which the oxidation ratio was determined by \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR of the digested sample (Figure
S3). Interestingly, the conversion of \(\mathsf{b t k}^{2-}\) to \(\mathsf{b t k}^{2-}\) in the crystals can only reach \(50\%\). Further increasing the reaction temperature and/or reaction time led to framework decomposition/collapse (Figure. S1). Note that MAF-23 cannot be oxidized when it was heated at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in oxygen even for 2000 min (Figure S3). Further, no crystalline product can be obtained so far by reactions of \(\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{I I})\) salts with pure \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t k}\) or mixed \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t m}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t k}\).
Single-crystal structure of MAF-23-O has been successfully measured, which is isostructural with MAF-23 (Figure S4 and Table S1). However, one of the two independent organic ligands changed from \(\mathsf{b t m}^{2-}\) to \(\mathsf{b t k}^{2-}\), as emphasized by the typical \(\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O}\) bond length of 1.205(9) \(\mathsf{A}_{\mathsf{v}}^{[14]}\) indicating a perfect selectivity of the oxidation reaction. Careful structural analysis revealed that the methylene group of the oxidized \(\mathsf{b t m}^{2-}\) bends toward the channel, giving more space for \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) attack (Figure 1). Because methylene group has a similar size with carbonyl group, the unit-cell volume, void ratio, and channel size/shape before and after oxidation are basically unchanged (Figure 1 and S5).
Thermogravimetry and Powder X-ray diffraction showed that MAF-23-O decomposes above \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figures S6-S7), which is lower than for MAF-23 \((480\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\),[13a] which can be attributed to the relatively high acidity and coordination ability, and also likely the low stability of \(\mathsf{b t k}^{2-}\). The \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm of MAF-23-O measured at \(195\ \mathrm{~K~}\) shows quasi-type-I characteristic (Figure S8). Using the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) uptake at \(F/P_{0}=0.95\) (liquid density of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\), \(1.101\ \mathfrak{g}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-3}\)), the pore volume can be calculated as \(0.183\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\), slightly larger than the crystallographic value of \(0.170\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\). For comparison, the pore volumes of MAF-23 calculated from the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) isotherm and the crystal structure were reported to be \(0.246\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.173\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively, which was ascribed to the framework expansion as confirmed by SCXRD.[13a] In this context, MAF-23-O has a smaller framework flexibility compared with MAF-23, at least in the thermodynamic point of view for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption.
Mixture breakthrough experiments can straightforwardly illustrate gas separation performances of adsorbents, but the adsorbate uptake and selectivity can be hardly calculated from the outlet concentrations. We measured the outlet flow rates in this study, the outlet flow rates were measured for the \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) breakthrough curves, so that integrating the breakthrough curves can give adsorbate retention at any time.[15] \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) breakthrough almost at the same time (ca. \(2.1\ \mathsf{m m o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\)) in the MAF-23 column (Figure 2a). In contrast, for MAF-23-O, the breakthrough point of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) (ca. \(0.7\ \mathsf{m m o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\)) is much earlier than that of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) (ca. \(2.4\ \mathsf{m m o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\)), and the latter is later than that for MAF-23 (Figures 2b and S9-S10), meaning that after oxidation, \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) adsorption is largely reduced and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) adsorption is enhanced.
After the breakthrough curves stabilize, the uptakes of MAF-23 and MAF-23-O for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) reach 1.23/0.85 and 1.30/0.09 mmol \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), corresponding to adsorption selectivity of 1.5 and 15, respectively (Table S2). For comparison, we also performed the same breakthrough measurements for Co-MOF-74 and KAUST-7 (Figures 2c-d, S9 and S11). The obtained breakthrough curves of Co-MOF-74 and KAUST-7 are similar with those reported in the literatures,[10d, 11] except that outlet flow rates were measured in this work. The \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) equilibrium uptakes were calculated as \(6.50/1.00\) and \(1.16/0.10\ \mathsf{m m o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\) for Co-MOF-74 and KAUST-7, giving selectivities of 6.5 and 12, respectively.
To study the \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) separation mechanisms of MAF-23 and MAF-23-O, single-component adsorption isotherms were measured at \(298\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and \(313\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure 3a). For MAF-23, the \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) isotherms and the calculated adsorption enthalpies are very similar (Figures S13-15), corresponding well with its poor separation performance. For MAF-23-O, the \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) isotherms have obvious difference, especially at low pressure. The \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) adsorption enthalpy \((54\,\,\mathsf{k J\,\,m o l^{-1}})\) is much higher than that of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) \((34\,\,\mathsf{k J\,\,m o l^{-1}})\) (Figures S16 and S17). The IAST (ideal adsorbed solution theory) selectivities were calculated as 8-9 for MAF-23-O and 3-4 for MAF-23 (Figures S18 and S19), which are somewhat lower and higher than those determined from the breakthrough experiments, respectively (Table S2). Note that IAST has many requirements, and large errors can arise from narrow pores (non-ideal gas solution), framework flexibility, large binding difference, etc.[10e,16] Therefore, the IAST selectivites were calculated here just for qualitative comparison (Table S3).
The high experimental \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) selectivity of MAF-23-O is also contributed by kinetic factors. During the measurement of gas adsorption isotherms, we noticed that the \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) sorption rates for MAF-23 were similar, but they were very different for MAF-23-O. The diffusional time constants \((D=D/r^{2})\) of MAF-23-O were calculated from its adsorption kinetic profiles
measured at 298 K (Figure 3b).[17] The kinetic selectivity \((D(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6})/D(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}))\) was calculated as 71, which is quite high but lower than many adsorbents famous with kinetic selectivity (Table S4). Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations were performed to study the diffusion behaviors (Figure S20 and Table S5). When the MOFs were set flexible, the diffusion coefficients of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) for MAF-23 and MAF-23-O were obtained as 3.3/2.8 and \(0.62/0.0073\times10^{-10}\,\mathsf{m}^{2}\,\mathsf{s}^{-1}\), respectively. The selectivity \((D(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6})/D(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}))\) of MAF-23-O (112.3) is almost one hundred times higher than for MAF-23 (1.2), which is consistent with the experimental result. When the MOFs were set rigid, \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) could not diffuse (Figure S21), confirming that the small aperture sizes of the MOFs need to transiently open for diffusion of the large guests (Figures S22 and S23).[18] While conventionally reported flexibility is reflected by the structure differences of the host at thermodynamic equilibrium states (thermodynamically controlled flexibility), this kind of (kinetically controlled) flexibility has little or no structure difference at the thermodynamic equilibrium states.[19] Snapshots of the MD results showed that, the transient structural transformations of the hosts for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) diffusion mainly occur at the ligand conformation, which changes the dihedral angles between the two aromatic rings (Figure S24). Periodic density functional theory (PDFT) calculations showed that, compared with MAF-23, MAF-23-O needs much higher energy increase for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) diffusion, because the carbonyl group has \(\pi\)-conjugation with the two aromatic rings. These results demonstrate that flexibility is necessary for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) adsorption in MAF-23-O, but the high kinetic selectivity is a result of the slow \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) diffusion or the low flexibility of the MOF
structure (Figures S25 and S26), further indicating that the preferential adsorption site is not so specific. In addition, we successfully measured the single-crystal X-ray structures of these host-guest systems, although the sample single-crystallinity was very difficult to retain after a series of postsynthetic treatments (heating, oxidation, adsorption, etc.).[22] As shown in Figure S27, positions of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) in MAF-23, and also \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) in MAF-23-O are consistent with the PDFT results. On the other hand, \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) in MAF-23-O is highly disordered and cannot be modeled, indicating the existence of multiple guest positions/orientations with similar and weak host-guest binding energies, which is consistent with the GCMC and MD results.
Ultimate thermodynamic or dynamic separation is always difficult to achieve. Our results show that although neither the thermodynamic nor kinetic selectivities of MAF-23-O is outstanding among reported MOFs, simultaneous improvement of them can give extraordinarily high practical adsorption selectivity.
